  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nsatellite imagery early this morning shows mainly clear skies \nacross the CWA for all but one strip of stratus between RWF and \nMKT. This has allowed temperatures to drop quickly into the \nupper 20s to low 30s for all except the Twin Cities urban heat \nisland and where the aforementioned clouds are still lingering \nin southern Minnesota. To the east over northern Wisconsin, \nthere are some clouds moving down off Lake Superior, but they \nhave made very little progress into western Wisconsin over the \npast several hours. As was mentioned in the previous AFD update,\nMinTs have been lowered to account for this loss of cloud cover\nthat models struggled to resolve for much of the day yesterday.\nFelt that CONSSHORT and NBM had a decent handle on both the sky\ncover and temps, so have used a blend of those to lower MinTs \nheading into this morning. As of 1:45am CST, MSP is sitting at \n34, which is the coldest temperature we've seen so far this \nseason. Given there is still plenty of time to drop a couple \nmore degrees before sunrise, this morning could very likely end \nup being the first freeze of the season for the Twin Cities \nmetro. \n\nAt the surface, a broad 1026mb high is centered over Minnesota. This \nwill allow for some sunshine to at least start the day following \nseveral days of gloomy, overcast conditions. Once again, cloud cover \nremains the biggest forecast uncertainty. As the high drifts to\nthe south through the day, the stratus that is currently \nrotating around the western periphery will come back into \nwestern Minnesota. This won't have much in the way of impacts on\nsensible weather, but if it does get into southwestern \nMinnesota (generally along the Minnesota River valley to MKT) \nearly enough, it could impact today's high temperatures. CAA \nwill continue into at least this evening, so highs are only \nexpected to be in the 40s anyway. Overnight into Saturday \nmorning, this cloud deck could work to keep temperatures a \nlittle warmer than this morning, when it had previously looked \nlike Saturday would be the colder of the two days. In either \ncase, this will be the last of the below freezing temperatures \nthrough at least the middle of next week. We'll see a warming \ntrend Saturday onward with highs making a run at the low 60s by \nMonday. A weak cold front will move through Monday night, but \ntemperatures will still be in the 50s for the remainder of the \nforecast period. Precip wise, we look to remain dry overall, but\na weak system during the Wednesday/Thursday timeframe may bring\nsome light shower chances to eastern portions of the forecast \narea.",
